Client & Franchise Outcomes — Enterprise Container Modernisation and Developer Enablement
Led strategic modernisation and developer enablement across Citi’s container ecosystem, delivering reusable enterprise capabilities that improve engineering productivity, accelerate adoption of approved technologies and embed security into the developer experience.
Enterprise container modernisation: Drove container base-image modernisation in partnership with Middleware Engineering across 14+ product image families and more than 100 distinct image variations, including Java, Python, Node.js, Apache and NGINX. Accelerated the delivery of approved runtime images, resolved compatibility challenges and advanced adoption of RHEL 9 UBI Minimal/Micro, automated base-image updates and regular image refreshes. RHEL 9 adoption across the container estate increased from 3.4% in March to 14.8%, while average image age fell from 185 to 98 days - a reduction of approximately 47%.
Systematically optimised product images through multi-stage builds, removal of unnecessary components and Java runtime optimisation using jlink, replacing large JDK installations with leaner runtimes where appropriate. These practices delivered approximately 110 MB of image-size reduction across around 80% of image families and contributed to average reductions of more than 60% in Critical CVEs and 70% in High CVEs across the base-image portfolio. Combined with Renovate adoption and more frequent image refreshes, these improvements strengthened image quality, reduced vulnerability exposure and enabled application teams to consume more current, optimised runtime images.
GenAI-powered developer enablement: Led the evolution of the Dockerfile Improvement Preset (DiP) from an early-stage concept into an enterprise developer capability within Citi’s Stylus Workspaces. Drove product evolution and adoption, aligning DiP with VSaaS Hard Gating as a self-service remediation path to help developers modernise Dockerfiles, adopt approved base images and apply Citi’s container engineering standards. Adoption has extended across more than 200 team repositories, supported by enterprise communications, developer sessions, TechCon, Cyber Week and Architecture & Engineering All Hands.
Took ownership of CHRIS (Container Hub Reporting & Image Scanning), an unplanned strategic initiative outside the original roadmap. Mobilised additional engineering capacity and established technical leadership to accelerate delivery. CHRIS is progressing through UAT towards production and is designed to provide a more holistic view of container inventories, image usage and security posture across CSIs, with a roadmap for integration across VSaaS, image ingestion, base-image capabilities and DiP.
Scalable platform and operator ingestion: Led the modernisation of the OpenShift Platform team’s operator and platform image-ingestion approach, designing and implementing a standardised solution aligned with Citi’s Third-Party Image Ingestion Pipeline (3PiiP). Partnered closely with Artifactory Engineering and Operations and the OpenShift Platform team to address digest-based image references and enable production adoption. The pipeline has executed approximately 3,500 times to ingest platform and operator images, extending 3PiiP established ingestion capability to a broader platform ecosystem and improving consistency, automation and scalability.
Engineering excellence and responsible AI adoption: Advanced engineering practices across the Container Security portfolio by driving responsible use of Devin, GitHub Copilot and CodeBot; strengthening architectural and project context through AGENTS.md; and integrating AI-assisted code reviews and automated dependency management through Renovate and Release-on-Demand (RoD). Complemented these changes with Python dependency-management modernisation from pip to uv and broader SDLC improvements, reducing VSaaS build time by more than 40%. This established a more consistent, efficient engineering operating model that combines automation and AI-enabled productivity with human judgement and engineering accountability.

